{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for lane B revision 2 at exact head e50b7b7de1d7e99b57a821f2969a13214cb6f2f2. Primary: notes/2026-10-01-laneB-git.md; all 45 submitted artifact bases are included. The five findings in review 50104b16 are addressed in their reported cases, but the remaining cases below prevent approval of the requested functionality.\n\nValidation: clean installation, repository type checks, 90 Node tests with real git/SQLite, and 8 workerd tests passed independently. Six new diagnostic tests reproduce the remaining defects. A seventh independent test compares merge bases with a complete ancestry oracle on 10,000 generated histories with skewed timestamps; every returned set matches exactly. Reviewed the source/test changes against d84a0a25 and the submitted live-run evidence. Existing unchanged artifacts retain the previous review's inspection, with their bytes compared at this head. No live deploy, token mint or measurement driver was run in this review, and no production sources were changed.\n\nPrior findings: the readiness revision and pending-evaluation reservation guard close P1.1; exact Artifacts namespace/source comparison closes P1.2 for an existing repository; delayed-mint/expiry checks close the demonstrated P1.3 timing failures; parent-link ancestry removal closes P2.4; level/path ordering closes P2.5's unstable refusal selection. Keep these tests and the strict stance on unresolved publication.\n\n1. [P1, Security/credential recovery] Persist fork inventory cleanup and creation-token revocation, not just known lease-token IDs. In packages/git/src/workspace/workspaces.ts, ensureFork revokes the fork's long-lived creation token directly; if that revocation fails, no durable cleanup record is written. The next provisioning call sees the valid fork, skips creation-token cleanup, mints a lease token, and reports ready with two active write tokens, including the 24-hour creation token. Independent reproduction confirms sweep returns zero with that token still active. Separately, revoke remembers only the recorded lease token before awaiting listTokens. If inventory fails, an unrecorded token from the documented mint-before-record crash window is not remembered. Once Artifacts recovers, sweep clears the known token and reports zero while the orphan remains active. Persist a fork-level inventory/revocation obligation before crossing these boundaries, and resolve it only after confirmed cleanup. Recover after failed inventory, failed creation-token revocation, lost responses, and restart. Retain positive provenance checks so cleanup never touches another repository. The candidate harness alarm currently reconciles only landing; wire and schedule workspace cleanup explicitly in the hosting Room rather than claiming the existing alarm already runs sweep.\n\n2. [P2, Architecture/lease fencing] Fence every asynchronous workspace result and cleanup by the lease it started for. The provisionNow catch calls fail(lane, error), which reads the newest row and marks it failed without comparing the original lease. A gated old fork-info failure after lease 2 opens marks lease 2 failed; the diagnostic confirms the returned failed op is for lease 2. A delayed revoke has the opposite race: after release starts for lease 1, lease 2 can provision and receive a token; the old listTokens then resumes, enumerates that new token, and revokes it. The lease-2 row remains ready and grant keeps returning the revoked credential. Both are reproduced through the public Workspaces API with gated Artifacts responses. Bind state writes and inventory cleanup to durable operation/lease identity; serialize cleanup and new credential installation or otherwise distinguish tokens owed by each lease. Test both orderings and restart, including late errors, late success and late revocation. A newer holder must not inherit an older operation's failure or lose its credential to old cleanup.\n\n3. [P2, Architecture/renewal] Update the deadline of a pending workspace when the same lease is renewed. Workspaces.open returns an existing pending row for the same generation without recording the new leaseExpiresAt. The diagnostic opens a 120-second lease, advances 61 seconds, then renews that generation for another 15 minutes. Provision still uses the old 59-second remainder and fails despite the valid renewed lease. Store the renewed deadline and make pending/in-flight minting read the current value; keep the after-await check against it. Test renewal before provisioning and during token creation, preserving the single valid credential and no credential beyond the current lease.\n\n4. [P2, Architecture/scalability] Enforce a work/memory bound while making deterministic tree-diff refusals. packages/git/src/diff/treediff.ts fetches the whole level with Promise.all before checking maxEntries. The diagnostic uses 100 child directories of 100 files and maxEntries 150: it fetches all 100 child trees and 10,000 child entries before refusing, while reported stats.entries is only 200. With production limits, a wide level can multiply reads and retained arrays far beyond the configured entry budget. Determinism should not require exhausting an already over-budget level. Process a stable ordered stream or bounded batches, limit outstanding/prefetched data, and stop scheduling once the deterministic budget is crossed. Preserve the previous refusal-order regression tests and add a wide-level work-bound test with truthful resource stats. If a separate fetch or byte budget is necessary, define and enforce it explicitly rather than treating the examined-entry count as protection against materializing the whole level.\n\nArchitecture: the durable readiness revision is a sound repair, and reservation still performs its synchronous comparison. Publication recovery continues to hold the slot for unknown pushes and use the same integration/expected main; do not weaken that rule. Workspace cleanup needs comparable durable ownership and fencing. Production Room integration remains a separate required seam; the harness is correctly identified as a stand-in.\n\nSecurity: exact fork provenance is a substantial improvement. Complete durable cleanup before claiming one lease-bound write token and revocation of all obsolete tokens. An inventory error or token's elapsed TTL is not a confirmed cleanup receipt. No token leak or deployed exploit is demonstrated by these local tests.\n\nSimplification: use one durable workspace cleanup protocol for creation tokens, orphan inventory and known lease-token IDs, with a clear Room alarm contract. Keep the deterministic diff traversal but bound its pipeline. Update the README Workspaces constructor example to include the now-required namespace.\n\nThe measured live result supports the documented harness loop and released-token refusal; it does not cover the overlapping operations or failure cases above. Full scope is retained, and schedule estimates are not acceptance limits.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f2236dfa558520a4c7b9ddb3a49952fa54394618","binding":"assigned","head":"e50b7b7de1d7e99b57a821f2969a13214cb6f2f2","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:03541fa24ac5915d42e5fbfe0fd59f5e720806ec\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f1c712fd424d1444df42240b8eeae16c41626171\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:03541fa24ac5915d42e5fbfe0fd59f5e720806ec","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}